+# Cause
+
+The note page itself is crawlable and returns HTTP 200, but its HTML is only a shell. `site/app.js` fetches the actual note from `/v1/notes/{slug}`. Since September 7, `site/robots.txt` has disallowed `/v1/`, so Google cannot fetch that data while rendering. The app treats any failed fetch as a missing note and displays “NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address” with HTTP 200. The October 16 live inspection for `/n/drip-line-pressure` shows exactly that rendered text and a Soft 404 verdict. Notes crawled before the robots change remain indexed; the four notes crawled in October are reported as Soft 404.
+
+# Fix
+
+In `site/robots.txt`, allow `/v1/notes`, which covers the public list (`/v1/notes?limit=50`) and individual note reads (`/v1/notes/{slug}`). Keep the general `/v1/` disallow, add the more specific disallow for `/v1/notes/*/reactions`, and retain `/admin/`. Under Google's longest matching rule, the public note reads are allowed while reactions and other API paths remain blocked. Robots rules apply to URLs, not HTTP methods, so they cannot separately forbid POST or PUT on an otherwise allowed note URL; those methods require bearer authorization according to `api/routes.md`.
+
+The colleague's central diagnosis applies: JavaScript rendering needs a public API path that robots.txt had blocked, and the resulting HTTP 200 error screen is a Soft 404. The longest matching rule approach also applies. The suggested homepage HTML links are a separate discovery improvement, not the cause of this Soft 404. The colleague's indexing counts, dates, routes, and post-deployment results do not describe this site. This change is local only; no live recrawl or indexing result is claimed.
